  }))), "Motivation"), mdx("p", null, "I chose this paper because it combines three things I\u2019m really interested in social media, models that work on the new topics without training, and LLMs that can \u201Cshow their work\u201D when they reason.\nFirst, social media is full of short, opinionated posts. Being able to tell if a post is for, against, or neutral about a topic is useful for understanding public opinion, spotting misinformation, and studying online conflict. I wanted to see how current NLP methods handle this kind of real, messy data from Twitter.\nSecond, I like zero-shot methods, where a model can deal with new topics it has never seen during training. This feels closer to real-world use than models that depend on large labeled datasets for every new topic.\nFinally, this paper uses chain-of-thought reasoning and self-consistency the same kinds of prompting tricks I use when working with LLMs. I was curious to see how researchers apply these ideas in a more systematic way: how they design the prompts, how they judge the reasoning, and whether this actually improves results.\nOverall, I picked this paper because it matches how I already like to use LLMs and gives me a concrete, research-based example of using reasoning-style prompting for a real NLP task."), mdx("h2", {
